              aallll       all messages
              ddeeffaauullttss  default messages (fatal,error,user,warning,info)
              ddeebbuugg     debug messages
              eerrrroorr     non‐fatal system errors (cannot be turned off)
              ffaattaall     fatal errors (cannot be turned off)
              iinnffoo      information
              mmaapp       map errors
              ssttaattss     additional statistical information
              uusseerr      non‐fatal user errors
              wwaarrnn      warnings
              wwaarrnniinngg   warnings


       mmaapp__rreellooaadd__iinntteerrvvaall (numeric, default=3600)
              The number of seconds that Amd will wait before it checks to see
              if any maps have changed at  their  source  (NIS  servers,  LDAP
              servers,  files,  etc.).   Amd  will reload only those maps that
              have changed.


       nnffss__aallllooww__aannyy__iinntteerrffaaccee (string, default=no)
              Normally Amd accepts local NFS packets only from 127.0.0.1.   If
              this  parameter  is  set to "yes" then Amd will accept local NFS
              packets from any local interface; this is useful on  hosts  that
              may  have multiple interfaces where the system is forced to send
              all outgoing packets (even those bound to the same host) via  an
              address other than 127.0.0.1.


       nnffss__aallllooww__iinnsseeccuurree__ppoorrtt (string, default=no)
              Normally Amd will refuse requests coming from unprivileged ports
              (i.e.   ports  >= 1024 on Unix systems), so that only privileged
              users and the kernel can send NFS requests to it.  However, some
              kernels (certain versions of Darwin, MacOS X,  and  Linux)  have
              bugs that cause them to use unprivileged ports in certain situa‐
              tions,  which causes Amd to stop dead in its tracks.  This para‐
              meter allows Amd to operate normally even on  such  systems,  at
              the  expense  of a slight decrease in the security of its opera‐
              tions.   If  you  see  messages  like  "ignoring  request   from
              foo:1234,  port not reserved" in your Amd log, try enabling this
              parameter and give it another go.


       nnffss__pprroottoo (string, default to trying version tcp then udp)
              By default, Amd tries TCP and then UDP.  This option forces  the
              overall  NFS  protocol used to TCP or UDP.  It overrides what is
              in the Amd maps, and is useful when Amd is compiled  with  NFSv3
              support  that  may not be stable.  With this option you can turn
              off the complete usage of NFSv3 dynamically (without  having  to
              recompile  Amd)  until  such  time  as  NFSv3 support is desired
              again.


       nnffss__rreettrraannssmmiitt__ccoouunntteerr (numeric, default=11)
              Same as the _r_e_t_r_a_n_s_m_i_t part of the --tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t_._r_e_t_r_a_n_s_m_i_t  option
              to  Amd.   Specifies  the number of NFS retransmissions that the
              kernel will use to communicate with Amd.


       nnffss__rreettrraannssmmiitt__ccoouunntteerr__uuddpp (numeric, default=11)
              Same as the  nnffss__rreettrraannssmmiitt__ccoouunntteerr  option,  but  for  all  UDP
              mounts only.


       nnffss__rreettrraannssmmiitt__ccoouunntteerr__ttccpp (numeric, default=11)
              Same  as  the  nnffss__rreettrraannssmmiitt__ccoouunntteerr  option,  but  for all TCP
              mounts only.


       nnffss__rreettrraannssmmiitt__ccoouunntteerr__ttooppllvvll (numeric, default=11)
              Same as the nnffss__rreettrraannssmmiitt__ccoouunntteerr option, but  only  for  Amd’s
              top‐level UDP mounts.


       nnffss__rreettrryy__iinntteerrvvaall (numeric, default=8)
              Same  as the _t_i_m_e_o_u_t part of the --tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t_._r_e_t_r_a_n_s_m_i_t option to
              Amd.  Specifies the NFS timeout interval, in _t_e_n_t_h_s of  seconds,
              between  NFS/RPC  retries  (for UDP and TCP).  This is the value
